{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347150368,"sessionID":"ses_f9e3438bdffe7KqbdMk08DJgkW","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ca166fc63dd545b8bef1b584","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/task.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/task.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n2: \n3: # Task\n4: \n5: Operator: **Sigmoid**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `sigmoid` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n6: \n7: # Operator specification\n8: \n9: ## desc.md\n10: \n11: # Sigmoid 算子 API 描述\n12: \n13: ## 1. 算子简介\n14: \n15: Sigmoid 算子对输入张量完成 Sigmoid 运算，将任意实数映射到 (0, 1) 区间。\n16: \n17: **主要应用场景**：\n18: - 二分类任务的输出层激活\n19: - 门控机制（LSTM、GRU 等 RNN 中的门控信号）\n20: - 注意力权重计算\n21: - 概率输出的归一化\n22: \n23: **算子特征**：\n24: - 难度等级：L1（Elementwise）\n25: - 单输入单输出，逐元素运算，输出 shape 与输入完全一致\n26: - 支持 ND 格式输入\n27: \n28: ## 2. 算子定义\n29: \n30: ### 数学公式\n31: \n32: $$\n33: y = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-x}}\n34: $$\n35: \n36: ### 特殊情况\n37: \n38: | 输入 | 输出 |\n39: |------|------|\n40: | x = 0 | y = 0.5 |\n41: | x → +∞ | y → 1 |\n42: | x → -∞ | y → 0 |\n43: \n44: ## 3. 接口规范\n45: \n46: ### 算子原型\n47: \n48: ```python\n49: cann_bench.sigmoid(Tensor x) -> Tensor y\n50: ```\n51: \n52: ### 输入参数说明\n53: \n54: | 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n55: |------|------|--------|------|\n56: | x | Tensor | 必选 | 输入张量，支持 ND 格式 |\n57: \n58: ### 输出\n59: \n60: | 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n61: |------|-------|-------|------|\n62: | y | 与输入 x 相同 | 与输入 x 相同 | Sigmoid 激活结果 |\n63: \n64: ### 数据类型\n65: \n66: | 输入 dtype | 输出 dtype |\n67: |-----------|-----------|\n68: | float16 | float16 |\n69: | float32 | float32 |\n70: | bfloat16 | bfloat16 |\n71: \n72: ### 规则与约束\n73: \n74: - 输出 shape 与输入 shape 完全一致，输出 dtype 与输入 dtype 一致\n75: - 无额外属性参数\n76: \n77: ### 支持范围\n78: \n79: 输入 tensor 各维度与参数的支持范围：\n80: \n81: | 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n82: |---|---|---|\n83: | `x` 维度数 (ndim) | 1 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 1 ~ 5 |\n84: | `x` 单维大小 | 1 ~ 16384 | cases.csv 实测 2 ~ 8193 |\n85: | `x` 总元素数 | 1 ~ 64M | cases.csv 实测 ~1M ~ 64M  |\n86: \n87: 约束：输出 shape 与输入 shape 完全一致，无 broadcasting；无额外属性。\n88: \n89: ## 4. 精度要求\n90: \n91: 采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n92: \n93: **误差指标**：\n94: \n95: 1. 平均相对误差（MERE）：采样点中相对误差平均值\n96: \n97:    $$\n98:    \\text{MERE} = \\text{avg}(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n99:    $$\n100: \n101: 2. 最大相对误差（MARE）：采样点中相对误差最大值\n102: \n103:    $$\n104:    \\text{MARE} = \\max(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n105:    $$\n106: \n107: **通过标准**：\n108: \n109: | 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 | HiFLOAT32 | FLOAT8 E4M3 | FLOAT8 E5M2 |\n110: |----------|---------|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n111: | **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 | 2^-11 | 2^-3 | 2^-2 |\n112: \n113: 当平均相对误差 MERE < Threshold，最大相对误差 MARE < 10 * Threshold 时判定为通过。\n114: \n115: \n116: ## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n117: \n118: ```python\n119: import torch\n120: \n121: def sigmoid(\n122:     x: torch.Tensor\n123: ) -> torch.Tensor:\n124:     \"\"\"\n125:     对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n126: \n127:     公式: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n128: \n129:     Args:\n130:         x: 输入张量\n131: \n132:     Returns:\n133:         输出张量，Sigmoid激活结果\n134:     \"\"\"\n135: \n136:     y = torch.sigmoid(x)\n137:     return y\n138: ```\n139: \n140: ## 6. 额外信息\n141: \n142: ### 算子调用示例\n143: \n144: ```python\n145: import torch\n146: import cann_bench\n147: \n148: x = torch.randn(1024, 1024, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\n149: y = cann_bench.sigmoid(x)\n150: ```\n151: \n152: ## proto.yaml\n153: \n154: ```yaml\n155: operator:\n156:   name: Sigmoid\n157:   category: Elementwise\n158:   difficulty: L1\n159:   formula: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n160:   description: 对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n161:   shape_support: 输入ND格式\n162:   attrs: []\n163:   inputs:\n164:   - name: x\n165:     description: 输入张量\n166:     dtype:\n167:     - float32\n168:     - float16\n169:     - bfloat16\n170:   outputs:\n171:   - name: y\n172:     description: 输出张量，Sigmoid激活结果\n173:     dtype:\n174:     - float32\n175:     - float16\n176:     - bfloat16\n177:   schema: sigmoid(Tensor x) -> Tensor y\n178: ```\n179: \n180: ## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n181: \n182: ```python\n183: #!/usr/bin/python3\n184: # coding=utf-8\n185: \n186: \n187: import torch\n188: \n189: \"\"\"\n190: Sigmoid算子Torch Golden参考实现\n191: \n192: 对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n193: 公式: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n194: \"\"\"\n195: def sigmoid(\n196:     x: torch.Tensor\n197: ) -> torch.Tensor:\n198:     \"\"\"\n199:     对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n200:     \n201:     公式: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n202:     \n203:     Args:\n204:         x: 输入张量\n205:     \n206:     Returns:\n207:         输出张量，Sigmoid激活结果\n208:     \"\"\"\n209: \n210:     y = torch.sigmoid(x)\n211:     return y\n212: ```\n213: \n214: ## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n215: \n216: | case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n217: |---|---|---|---|---|\n218: | 1 | [[1024, 1024]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 1] | - |\n219: | 2 | [[2048, 2048]] | ['float32'] | [-2, 2] | - |\n220: | 3 | [[4096, 4096]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 3] | - |\n221: | 4 | [[8192, 8192]] | ['float16'] | [-10, 10] | - |\n222: | 5 | [[8192, 8192]] | ['float32'] | [-100, 100] | - |\n223: | 6 | [[1023, 1023]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | - |\n224: | 7 | [[1009, 1021]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 2] | - |\n225: | 8 | [[1537, 769]] | ['float32'] | [-5, 10] | - |\n226: | 9 | [[363, 367, 373]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-50, 100] | - |\n227: | 10 | [[2049, 513]] | ['float16'] | [-65504, 65504] | - |\n228: | 11 | [[3, 7, 13, 4001]] | ['float32'] | [-88, 88] | - |\n229: | 12 | [[1000003]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-inf, inf] | - |\n230: | 13 | [[11, 13, 17, 67, 67]] | ['float32'] | [nan, nan] | - |\n231: | 14 | [[3, 7, 11, 13, 1009]] | ['float16'] | [0, 0] | - |\n232: | 15 | [[512, 2049]] | ['float32'] | [-0.5, 0.5] | - |\n233: | 16 | [[255, 8193]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-1, 3] | - |\n234: | 17 | [[4097, 511]] | ['float16'] | [-1000, 1000] | - |\n235: | 18 | [[2, 511, 2049]] | ['float32'] | [-0.2, 0.2] | - |\n236: | 19 | [[4, 255, 2049]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 6] | - |\n237: | 20 | [[2, 3, 17, 1024, 101]] | ['float32'] | [-20, 40] | - |\n238: \n239: # Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n240: \n241: ```python\n242: \"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n243: \n244: Kernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n245:   - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n246:   - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n247:   - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n248:     (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n249:   - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n250:     the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n251:     maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n252:   - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n253:     limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n254: \"\"\"\n255: \n256: import torch\n257: \n258: import asc\n259: import asc2\n260: \n261: from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n262: \n263: _WIDE_TILE = 3072\n264: _NARROW_TILE = 1024\n265: _MAX_CORES = 72\n266: \n267: \n268: @asc2.jit\n269: def _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n270:                     size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n271:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n272:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n273:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n274:                         unroll_factor=2):\n275:         off = t * tile_size\n276:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n277:         x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n278:         xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n279:         y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n280:         asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n281: \n282: \n283: def sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n284:     \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n285:     ensure_npu_platform()\n286:     if not x.is_contiguous():\n287:         x = x.contiguous()\n288:     out = torch.empty_like(x)\n289:     size = x.numel()\n290:     if size == 0:\n291:         return out\n292:     if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n293:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n294:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n295:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n296:     else:\n297:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n298:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n299:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n300:     return out\n301: \n302: ```\n303: \n304: # pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n305: \n306: ## Module shape\n307: \n308: Your file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n309: \n310: - imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n311:   `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n312: - one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n313: - ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n314: - wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n315:   needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n316:   `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n317:   launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n318:   return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n319: \n320: ## Kernel authoring rules\n321: \n322: - Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n323:   `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n324:   copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n325: - Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n326:   (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n327:   value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n328: - Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n329: \n330: ```python\n331: for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n332:     off = t * tile_size\n333:     n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n334:     x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n335:     ...compute on tiles...\n336:     asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n337: ```\n338: \n339: - Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n340:   on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n341: - Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n342:   `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n343:   `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n344:   `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n345:   comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n346:   `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n347:   `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n348:   `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n349:   `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n350:   `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n351:   `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n352: - int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n353:   (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n354:   no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n355: - Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n356:   write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n357: - f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n358:   compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n359: - UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n360:   Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n361:   doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n362:   calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n363:   `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n364:   311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n365:   `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n366:   Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n367:   512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n368:   bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n369:   cases).\n370: - `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n371:   (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n372: - Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n373:   `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n374:   (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n375:   different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n376:   `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n377:   `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n378:   TypeError).\n379: - Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n380:   [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n381:   int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n382:   destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n383:   Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n384:   `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n385:   kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n386: - Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n387:   `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n388:   style (min 32 bytes).\n389: - Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n390:   `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n391:   `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n392: \n393: ## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n394: \n395: - Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n396:   nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n397:   `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0 below tiny < 6e-8).\n398: - Proven cancellation-free building blocks (all verified on this harness):\n399:   - `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s, 0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\n400:   - `1 + tanh(u) = 2 * sigmoid(2u)`\n401:   - `tanh(softplus(x))`: with `w = exp(-|x|)`, equals\n402:     `(1 + 2w) / (1 + 2w + 2w^2)` for `x >= 0`, `(w^2 + 2w) / (w^2 + 2w + 2)`\n403:     for `x < 0` (exact identities; blend with `asc2.where(xf >= 0.0, ...)`)\n404:   - `erfc(z)` for `z >= 0`: Numerical Recipes fit `t * exp(-z*z + P(t))`,\n405:     `t = 1/(1 + z/2)`, rel. err < 1.2e-7 (see the gelu reference module for\n406:     the 9-coefficient Horner chain)\n407: - IEEE special values (inf/nan scalars or extreme inputs) propagate correctly\n408:   through the hardware ops — do NOT special-case them with host branches\n409:   unless the golden does.\n410: \n411: ## Anti-cheat (violations zero the submission)\n412: \n413: - ALL numerical work happens inside `@asc2.jit` kernels launched on the NPU.\n414: - torch usage is allowed ONLY for: output allocation (`torch.empty`,\n415:   `torch.empty_like`), metadata (`.shape`, `.numel()`, `.stride()`, `.dtype`,\n416:   `.is_contiguous()`), contiguity (`.contiguous()`), and views (`.view`,\n417:   `.reshape`, `.narrow`, indexing that returns a view).\n418: - FORBIDDEN anywhere in the module: torch math/compute ops (`torch.mul`,\n419:   `torch.norm`, `torch.nn.functional.*`, tensor arithmetic like `a + b`,\n420:   `x.sigmoid()`, `.to(dtype)` casts of device data, `torch.cat`,\n421:   `torch.clone`, `torch.sum`, ...). The harness hooks torch dispatch and\n422:   rotates input data pointers between calls — caching outputs by `data_ptr`\n423:   is detected and scored as cheating.\n424: - Outputs must be contiguous NPU tensors with exactly the golden's\n425:   shape/dtype. Do not return views of inputs.\n426: \n427: \n428: # Operator-specific guidance\n429: \n430: -\n431: \n432: # Deliverable\n433: \n434: Write ONLY `candidate.py` (complete, self-contained module). Public callable named exactly `sigmoid` with the exact schema signature including attr defaults. Think hard about numerical stability across the full value ranges listed above, and about the UB budget for your op chain before picking TILE.\n435: \n436: IMPORTANT — no local execution: pyasc/asc/asc2/torch_npu are NOT installed on this machine and there is no NPU here. Do NOT run, import, compile-check, or test your code, and do not install packages — any such attempt wastes your entire time budget. Reason statically, write `candidate.py`, optionally run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` (syntax only), then STOP and reply DONE.\n\n(End of file - total 436 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n\n# Task\n\nOperator: **Sigmoid**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `sigmoid` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n\n# Operator specification\n\n## desc.md\n\n# Sigmoid 算子 API 描述\n\n## 1. 算子简介\n\nSigmoid 算子对输入张量完成 Sigmoid 运算，将任意实数映射到 (0, 1) 区间。\n\n**主要应用场景**：\n- 二分类任务的输出层激活\n- 门控机制（LSTM、GRU 等 RNN 中的门控信号）\n- 注意力权重计算","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/task.md","text":"You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n\n# Task\n\nOperator: **Sigmoid**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `sigmoid` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n\n# Operator specification\n\n## desc.md\n\n# Sigmoid 算子 API 描述\n\n## 1. 算子简介\n\nSigmoid 算子对输入张量完成 Sigmoid 运算，将任意实数映射到 (0, 1) 区间。\n\n**主要应用场景**：\n- 二分类任务的输出层激活\n- 门控机制（LSTM、GRU 等 RNN 中的门控信号）\n- 注意力权重计算\n- 概率输出的归一化\n\n**算子特征**：\n- 难度等级：L1（Elementwise）\n- 单输入单输出，逐元素运算，输出 shape 与输入完全一致\n- 支持 ND 格式输入\n\n## 2. 算子定义\n\n### 数学公式\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-x}}\n$$\n\n### 特殊情况\n\n| 输入 | 输出 |\n|------|------|\n| x = 0 | y = 0.5 |\n| x → +∞ | y → 1 |\n| x → -∞ | y → 0 |\n\n## 3. 接口规范\n\n### 算子原型\n\n```python\ncann_bench.sigmoid(Tensor x) -> Tensor y\n```\n\n### 输入参数说明\n\n| 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n|------|------|--------|------|\n| x | Tensor | 必选 | 输入张量，支持 ND 格式 |\n\n### 输出\n\n| 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n|------|-------|-------|------|\n| y | 与输入 x 相同 | 与输入 x 相同 | Sigmoid 激活结果 |\n\n### 数据类型\n\n| 输入 dtype | 输出 dtype |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| float16 | float16 |\n| float32 | float32 |\n| bfloat16 | bfloat16 |\n\n### 规则与约束\n\n- 输出 shape 与输入 shape 完全一致，输出 dtype 与输入 dtype 一致\n- 无额外属性参数\n\n### 支持范围\n\n输入 tensor 各维度与参数的支持范围：\n\n| 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `x` 维度数 (ndim) | 1 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 1 ~ 5 |\n| `x` 单维大小 | 1 ~ 16384 | cases.csv 实测 2 ~ 8193 |\n| `x` 总元素数 | 1 ~ 64M | cases.csv 实测 ~1M ~ 64M  |\n\n约束：输出 shape 与输入 shape 完全一致，无 broadcasting；无额外属性。\n\n## 4. 精度要求\n\n采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n\n**误差指标**：\n\n1. 平均相对误差（MERE）：采样点中相对误差平均值\n\n   $$\n   \\text{MERE} = \\text{avg}(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n   $$\n\n2. 最大相对误差（MARE）：采样点中相对误差最大值\n\n   $$\n   \\text{MARE} = \\max(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n   $$\n\n**通过标准**：\n\n| 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 | HiFLOAT32 | FLOAT8 E4M3 | FLOAT8 E5M2 |\n|----------|---------|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 | 2^-11 | 2^-3 | 2^-2 |\n\n当平均相对误差 MERE < Threshold，最大相对误差 MARE < 10 * Threshold 时判定为通过。\n\n\n## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef sigmoid(\n    x: torch.Tensor\n) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"\n    对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n\n    公式: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n\n    Args:\n        x: 输入张量\n\n    Returns:\n        输出张量，Sigmoid激活结果\n    \"\"\"\n\n    y = torch.sigmoid(x)\n    return y\n```\n\n## 6. 额外信息\n\n### 算子调用示例\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport cann_bench\n\nx = torch.randn(1024, 1024, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\ny = cann_bench.sigmoid(x)\n```\n\n## proto.yaml\n\n```yaml\noperator:\n  name: Sigmoid\n  category: Elementwise\n  difficulty: L1\n  formula: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n  description: 对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n  shape_support: 输入ND格式\n  attrs: []\n  inputs:\n  - name: x\n    description: 输入张量\n    dtype:\n    - float32\n    - float16\n    - bfloat16\n  outputs:\n  - name: y\n    description: 输出张量，Sigmoid激活结果\n    dtype:\n    - float32\n    - float16\n    - bfloat16\n  schema: sigmoid(Tensor x) -> Tensor y\n```\n\n## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/python3\n# coding=utf-8\n\n\nimport torch\n\n\"\"\"\nSigmoid算子Torch Golden参考实现\n\n对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n公式: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n\"\"\"\ndef sigmoid(\n    x: torch.Tensor\n) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"\n    对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n    \n    公式: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n    \n    Args:\n        x: 输入张量\n    \n    Returns:\n        输出张量，Sigmoid激活结果\n    \"\"\"\n\n    y = torch.sigmoid(x)\n    return y\n```\n\n## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n\n| case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [[1024, 1024]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 1] | - |\n| 2 | [[2048, 2048]] | ['float32'] | [-2, 2] | - |\n| 3 | [[4096, 4096]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 3] | - |\n| 4 | [[8192, 8192]] | ['float16'] | [-10, 10] | - |\n| 5 | [[8192, 8192]] | ['float32'] | [-100, 100] | - |\n| 6 | [[1023, 1023]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | - |\n| 7 | [[1009, 1021]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 2] | - |\n| 8 | [[1537, 769]] | ['float32'] | [-5, 10] | - |\n| 9 | [[363, 367, 373]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-50, 100] | - |\n| 10 | [[2049, 513]] | ['float16'] | [-65504, 65504] | - |\n| 11 | [[3, 7, 13, 4001]] | ['float32'] | [-88, 88] | - |\n| 12 | [[1000003]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-inf, inf] | - |\n| 13 | [[11, 13, 17, 67, 67]] | ['float32'] | [nan, nan] | - |\n| 14 | [[3, 7, 11, 13, 1009]] | ['float16'] | [0, 0] | - |\n| 15 | [[512, 2049]] | ['float32'] | [-0.5, 0.5] | - |\n| 16 | [[255, 8193]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-1, 3] | - |\n| 17 | [[4097, 511]] | ['float16'] | [-1000, 1000] | - |\n| 18 | [[2, 511, 2049]] | ['float32'] | [-0.2, 0.2] | - |\n| 19 | [[4, 255, 2049]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 6] | - |\n| 20 | [[2, 3, 17, 1024, 101]] | ['float32'] | [-20, 40] | - |\n\n# Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n\n```python\n\"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n\nKernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n  - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n  - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n  - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n    (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n  - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n    the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n    maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n  - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n    limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport torch\n\nimport asc\nimport asc2\n\nfrom ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n\n_WIDE_TILE = 3072\n_NARROW_TILE = 1024\n_MAX_CORES = 72\n\n\n@asc2.jit\ndef _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n                    size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n    out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n    for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n                        unroll_factor=2):\n        off = t * tile_size\n        n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n        x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n        xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n        y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n        asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n\n\ndef sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n    ensure_npu_platform()\n    if not x.is_contiguous():\n        x = x.contiguous()\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    size = x.numel()\n    if size == 0:\n        return out\n    if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n    else:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n    return out\n\n```\n\n# pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n\n## Module shape\n\nYour file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n\n- imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n  `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n- one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n- ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n- wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n  needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n  `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n  launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n  return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n\n## Kernel authoring rules\n\n- Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n  `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n  copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n- Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n  (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n  value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n- Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n\n```python\nfor t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n    off = t * tile_size\n    n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n    x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n    ...compute on tiles...\n    asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n```\n\n- Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n  on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n- Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n  `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n  `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n  `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n  comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n  `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n  `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n  `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n  `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n  `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n  `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n- int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n  (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n  no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n- Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n  write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n- f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n  compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n- UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n  Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n  doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n  calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n  `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n  311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n  `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n  Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n  512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n  bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n  cases).\n- `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n  (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n- Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n  `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n  (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n  different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n  `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n  `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n  TypeError).\n- Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n  [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n  int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n  destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n  Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n  `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n  kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n- Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n  `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n  style (min 32 bytes).\n- Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n  `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n  `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n\n## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n\n- Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n  nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n  `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0 below tiny < 6e-8).\n- Proven cancellation-free building blocks (all verified on this harness):\n  - `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s, 0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\n  - `1 + tanh(u) = 2 * sigmoid(2u)`\n  - `tanh(softplus(x))`: with `w = exp(-|x|)`, equals\n    `(1 + 2w) / (1 + 2w + 2w^2)` for `x >= 0`, `(w^2 + 2w) / (w^2 + 2w + 2)`\n    for `x < 0` (exact identities; blend with `asc2.where(xf >= 0.0, ...)`)\n  - `erfc(z)` for `z >= 0`: Numerical Recipes fit `t * exp(-z*z + P(t))`,\n    `t = 1/(1 + z/2)`, rel. err < 1.2e-7 (see the gelu reference module for\n    the 9-coefficient Horner chain)\n- IEEE special values (inf/nan scalars or extreme inputs) propagate correctly\n  through the hardware ops — do NOT special-case them with host branches\n  unless the golden does.\n\n## Anti-cheat (violations zero the submission)\n\n- ALL numerical work happens inside `@asc2.jit` kernels launched on the NPU.\n- torch usage is allowed ONLY for: output allocation (`torch.empty`,\n  `torch.empty_like`), metadata (`.shape`, `.numel()`, `.stride()`, `.dtype`,\n  `.is_contiguous()`), contiguity (`.contiguous()`), and views (`.view`,\n  `.reshape`, `.narrow`, indexing that returns a view).\n- FORBIDDEN anywhere in the module: torch math/compute ops (`torch.mul`,\n  `torch.norm`, `torch.nn.functional.*`, tensor arithmetic like `a + b`,\n  `x.sigmoid()`, `.to(dtype)` casts of device data, `torch.cat`,\n  `torch.clone`, `torch.sum`, ...). The harness hooks torch dispatch and\n  rotates input data pointers between calls — caching outputs by `data_ptr`\n  is detected and scored as cheating.\n- Outputs must be contiguous NPU tensors with exactly the golden's\n  shape/dtype. Do not return views of inputs.\n\n\n# Operator-specific guidance\n\n-\n\n# Deliverable\n\nWrite ONLY `candidate.py` (complete, self-contained module). Public callable named exactly `sigmoid` with the exact schema signature including attr defaults. Think hard about numerical stability across the full value ranges listed above, and about the UB budget for your op chain before picking TILE.\n\nIMPORTANT — no local execution: pyasc/asc/asc2/torch_npu are NOT installed on this machine and there is no NPU here. Do NOT run, import, compile-check, or test your code, and do not install packages — any such attempt wastes your entire time budget. Reason statically, write `candidate.py`, optionally run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` (syntax only), then STOP and reply DONE.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":436,"totalLines":436,"truncated":false}},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/task.md","time":{"start":1788347150353,"end":1788347150365}},"id":"prt_061cbee91001TJ7ymcBMZNYat9","sessionID":"ses_f9e3438bdffe7KqbdMk08DJgkW","messageID":"msg_061cbdf2c001fsJa2FKMB95ZK8"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347150775,"sessionID":"ses_f9e3438bdffe7KqbdMk08DJgkW","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_cb0d0d6f2a634c7481e672e3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/design.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/design.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Design: Sigmoid (pyasc asc2 CANNBench kernel)\n2: \n3: ## Algorithm\n4: \n5: `y = 1 / (1 + exp(-x))`, all compute promoted to f32 inside the kernel.\n6: \n7: **Naive formula is safe here.** IEEE saturation handles extremes correctly:\n8: - `x → +∞`: `exp(-x) → 0` → `y = 1.0`\n9: - `x → -∞`: `exp(-x) → +inf` → `1/(inf+1) = 0.0`\n10: - `x = NaN`: propagates through as NaN\n11: - `x = 0`: `exp(0) = 1` → `y = 0.5`\n12: \n13: The numerically-stable split (`exp(min(x,0)) / (1+exp(-|x|))`) is unnecessary — the\n14: reference module using the naive formula scores 100% accuracy across all 20 cases,\n15: including f32 ranges [-88,88] and [-100,100] where `exp(100)` overflows f32 to inf but\n16: IEEE gives the correct answer (0). For f16 [-65504,65504] the same holds after f32\n17: promotion.\n18: \n19: ## Pinned-v2 API surface\n20: \n21: | Operation | API |\n22: |---|---|\n23: | Global memory view | `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) |\n24: | Tile load | `asc2.copy_in(gm, [off], [TILE], real_shape=[n])` |\n25: | Dtype promote | `x.to(asc.float32)` |\n26: | Negate | `-xf` (unary) |\n27: | Exponential | `asc2.exp(tile)` |\n28: | Add scalar | `tile + 1.0` (scalar on right) |\n29: | Divide | `asc2.div(1.0, tile)` |\n30: | Cast back | `y.to(x.dtype)` via `asc2.cast` |\n31: | Tile store | `asc2.copy_out(tile, gm, [off], real_shape=[n])` |\n32: | Grid-stride loop | `asc2.range(block_idx(), num_tiles, block_num(), unroll_factor=2)` |\n33: \n34: No `tensor`/`load`/`store` legacy spelling. Kernel params: `asc.GlobalAddress` for\n35: pointers, `int` for runtime sizes, `asc.ConstExpr[int]` for tile dimensions.\n36: \n37: ## All 20 cases\n38: \n39: | # | Shape | numel | dtype | range | Tail? |\n40: |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n41: | 1 | 1024×1024 | 1.05M | f16 | [-1,1] | no |\n42: | 2 | 2048×2048 | 4.19M | f32 | [-2,2] | no |\n43: | 3 | 4096×4096 | 16.78M | bf16 | [-3,3] | no |\n44: | 4 | 8192×8192 | 67.11M | f16 | [-10,10] | no |\n45: | 5 | 8192×8192 | 67.11M | f32 | [-100,100] | no |\n46: | 6 | 1023×1023 | 1.05M | bf16 | [-0.1,0.1] | yes |\n47: | 7 | 1009×1021 | 1.03M | f16 | [-1,2] | yes |\n48: | 8 | 1537×769 | 1.18M | f32 | [-5,10] | yes |\n49: | 9 | 363×367×373 | 49.69M | bf16 | [-50,100] | yes |\n50: | 10 | 2049×513 | 1.05M | f16 | [-65504,65504] | yes |\n51: | 11 | 3×7×13×4001 | 1.09M | f32 | [-88,88] | yes |\n52: | 12 | 1000003 | 1.00M | bf16 | [-inf,inf] | yes |\n53: | 13 | 11×13×17×67×67 | 10.91M | f32 | [nan,nan] | yes |\n54: | 14 | 3×7×11×13×1009 | 3.03M | f16 | [0,0] | yes |\n55: | 15 | 512×2049 | 1.05M | f32 | [-0.5,0.5] | yes |\n56: | 16 | 255×8193 | 2.09M | bf16 | [-1,3] | yes |\n57: | 17 | 4097×511 | 2.09M | f16 | [-1000,1000] | yes |\n58: | 18 | 2×511×2049 | 2.09M | f32 | [-0.2,0.2] | yes |\n59: | 19 | 4×255×2049 | 2.09M | bf16 | [-3,6] | yes |\n60: | 20 | 2×3×17×1024×101 | 10.55M | f32 | [-20,40] | yes |\n61: \n62: All cases flatten to 1-D via `numel()`. No broadcasting, no attributes.\n63: \n64: ## Tiling strategy\n65: \n66: Two tile sizes selected by host dispatch:\n67: - **Wide tile `TILE=3072`**: when `numel >= 72 * 3072 = 221184`. Covers cases 1–20\n68:   (minimum numel ~1M >> 221K).\n69: - **Narrow tile `TILE=1024`**: fallback for shapes below the threshold (none in this\n70:   set, but included for robustness).\n71: \n72: Grid-stride loop: `asc2.range(block_idx(), num_tiles, block_num(), unroll_factor=2)`.\n73: Cores: `min(72, num_tiles)`. 72 vector cores on Ascend 950PR.\n74: \n75: ## Tail handling\n76: \n77: ```python\n78: n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n79: ```\n80: Passed as `real_shape=[n]` to both `copy_in` and `copy_out`. No host-side padding,\n81: no shape mutation. Ternary expression for the tail condition is supported syntax.\n82: \n83: ## UB budget\n84: \n85: Op chain visible f32 tiles: `xf`, `neg_xf`, `exp_val`, `denom`, `result` = 5 tiles.\n86: With `unroll_factor=2` and measured 1.6× overhead factor:\n87: \n88: | TILE | Estimated UB | Status |\n89: |---|---|---|\n90: | 1024 | ~52K B | safe |\n91: | 2048 | ~156K B (measured: 155648) | safe |\n92: | 3072 | ~233K B | safe (< 253952) |\n93: | 4096 | ~311K B | **OVERFLOW** |\n94: \n95: **Selected: TILE=3072 (wide), TILE=1024 (narrow).** Both fit within the 253952 B\n96: budget.\n97: \n98: ## Numerical risks\n99: \n100: | Risk | Mitigation |\n101: |---|---|\n102: | `exp(-x)` overflow for large negative x | IEEE `inf` → `1/inf = 0`, matches golden |\n103: | f16 denormals near 0 | f32 promotion gives full mantissa range |\n104: | NaN propagation (case 13) | IEEE NaN passes through exp/div correctly |\n105: | ±inf (case 12) | bf16 ±inf → f32 ±inf → sigmoid gives 1.0/0.0 |\n106: | Catastrophic cancellation | None — no subtraction of near-equals in this formula |\n107: | Precision thresholds | f32 internal compute exceeds f16 threshold 2^-10, bf16 2^-7, f32 2^-13 |\n108: \n109: ## Anti-cheat constraints\n110: \n111: - All math in `@asc2.jit` kernel on NPU\n112: - `torch` used only for: `torch.empty_like(x)`, `x.numel()`, `x.is_contiguous()`,\n113:   `x.contiguous()`, `x.dtype`\n114: - No `torch.sigmoid`, no tensor arithmetic, no `.to(dtype)` on device tensors\n115: - No output caching, no `data_ptr` reuse\n116: - Output is a fresh contiguous tensor with golden's exact shape/dtype\n117: \n118: ## Host dispatch\n119: \n120: ```python\n121: def sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n122:     ensure_npu_platform()\n123:     x = x.contiguous() if not x.is_contiguous() else x\n124:     out = torch.empty_like(x)\n125:     size = x.numel()\n126:     if size == 0: return out\n127:     if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n128:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n129:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n130:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n131:     else:\n132:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n133:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n134:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n135:     return out\n136: ```\n137: \n138: ## Syntax compliance (per pyasc-syntax-constraints)\n139: \n140: - `for` loop with `asc2.range()`, no `break`/`continue`/`return`\n141: - Ternary `if` for tail condition (supported)\n142: - No `print`, no imports, no `try/except`, no `lambda`, no `math.*`\n143: - Scalars on the right of tile operations (`exp_val + 1.0`, not `1.0 + exp_val`)\n144: - Device functions may `return`; kernel writes to output tensors\n145: - `asc.ConstExpr[int]` for `tile_size` parameter (compile-time)\n146: \n147: ## Local validation ladder\n148: \n149: 1. `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` — syntax gate only\n150: 2. Worker static contract check — schema, imports, anti-cheat\n151: 3. Exact-v2 compile gate for all 20 cases — `verified-local-compile` label\n152: 4. **No numerical claims** without camodel (`verified-camodel`) or CANNBench\n153:    (`verified-cannbench`) execution\n154: \n155: DESIGN_DONE\n\n(End of file - total 155 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Design: Sigmoid (pyasc asc2 CANNBench kernel)\n\n## Algorithm\n\n`y = 1 / (1 + exp(-x))`, all compute promoted to f32 inside the kernel.\n\n**Naive formula is safe here.** IEEE saturation handles extremes correctly:\n- `x → +∞`: `exp(-x) → 0` → `y = 1.0`\n- `x → -∞`: `exp(-x) → +inf` → `1/(inf+1) = 0.0`\n- `x = NaN`: propagates through as NaN\n- `x = 0`: `exp(0) = 1` → `y = 0.5`\n\nThe numerically-stable split (`exp(min(x,0)) / (1+exp(-|x|))`) is unnecessary — the\nreference module using the naive formula scores 100% accuracy across all 20 cases,\nincluding f32 ranges [-88,88] and [-100,100] where `exp(100)` overflows f32 to inf but\nIEEE gives the correct answer (0). For f16 [-65504,65504] the same holds after f32\npromotion.\n\n## Pinned-v2 API surface\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/design.md","text":"# Design: Sigmoid (pyasc asc2 CANNBench kernel)\n\n## Algorithm\n\n`y = 1 / (1 + exp(-x))`, all compute promoted to f32 inside the kernel.\n\n**Naive formula is safe here.** IEEE saturation handles extremes correctly:\n- `x → +∞`: `exp(-x) → 0` → `y = 1.0`\n- `x → -∞`: `exp(-x) → +inf` → `1/(inf+1) = 0.0`\n- `x = NaN`: propagates through as NaN\n- `x = 0`: `exp(0) = 1` → `y = 0.5`\n\nThe numerically-stable split (`exp(min(x,0)) / (1+exp(-|x|))`) is unnecessary — the\nreference module using the naive formula scores 100% accuracy across all 20 cases,\nincluding f32 ranges [-88,88] and [-100,100] where `exp(100)` overflows f32 to inf but\nIEEE gives the correct answer (0). For f16 [-65504,65504] the same holds after f32\npromotion.\n\n## Pinned-v2 API surface\n\n| Operation | API |\n|---|---|\n| Global memory view | `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) |\n| Tile load | `asc2.copy_in(gm, [off], [TILE], real_shape=[n])` |\n| Dtype promote | `x.to(asc.float32)` |\n| Negate | `-xf` (unary) |\n| Exponential | `asc2.exp(tile)` |\n| Add scalar | `tile + 1.0` (scalar on right) |\n| Divide | `asc2.div(1.0, tile)` |\n| Cast back | `y.to(x.dtype)` via `asc2.cast` |\n| Tile store | `asc2.copy_out(tile, gm, [off], real_shape=[n])` |\n| Grid-stride loop | `asc2.range(block_idx(), num_tiles, block_num(), unroll_factor=2)` |\n\nNo `tensor`/`load`/`store` legacy spelling. Kernel params: `asc.GlobalAddress` for\npointers, `int` for runtime sizes, `asc.ConstExpr[int]` for tile dimensions.\n\n## All 20 cases\n\n| # | Shape | numel | dtype | range | Tail? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1024×1024 | 1.05M | f16 | [-1,1] | no |\n| 2 | 2048×2048 | 4.19M | f32 | [-2,2] | no |\n| 3 | 4096×4096 | 16.78M | bf16 | [-3,3] | no |\n| 4 | 8192×8192 | 67.11M | f16 | [-10,10] | no |\n| 5 | 8192×8192 | 67.11M | f32 | [-100,100] | no |\n| 6 | 1023×1023 | 1.05M | bf16 | [-0.1,0.1] | yes |\n| 7 | 1009×1021 | 1.03M | f16 | [-1,2] | yes |\n| 8 | 1537×769 | 1.18M | f32 | [-5,10] | yes |\n| 9 | 363×367×373 | 49.69M | bf16 | [-50,100] | yes |\n| 10 | 2049×513 | 1.05M | f16 | [-65504,65504] | yes |\n| 11 | 3×7×13×4001 | 1.09M | f32 | [-88,88] | yes |\n| 12 | 1000003 | 1.00M | bf16 | [-inf,inf] | yes |\n| 13 | 11×13×17×67×67 | 10.91M | f32 | [nan,nan] | yes |\n| 14 | 3×7×11×13×1009 | 3.03M | f16 | [0,0] | yes |\n| 15 | 512×2049 | 1.05M | f32 | [-0.5,0.5] | yes |\n| 16 | 255×8193 | 2.09M | bf16 | [-1,3] | yes |\n| 17 | 4097×511 | 2.09M | f16 | [-1000,1000] | yes |\n| 18 | 2×511×2049 | 2.09M | f32 | [-0.2,0.2] | yes |\n| 19 | 4×255×2049 | 2.09M | bf16 | [-3,6] | yes |\n| 20 | 2×3×17×1024×101 | 10.55M | f32 | [-20,40] | yes |\n\nAll cases flatten to 1-D via `numel()`. No broadcasting, no attributes.\n\n## Tiling strategy\n\nTwo tile sizes selected by host dispatch:\n- **Wide tile `TILE=3072`**: when `numel >= 72 * 3072 = 221184`. Covers cases 1–20\n  (minimum numel ~1M >> 221K).\n- **Narrow tile `TILE=1024`**: fallback for shapes below the threshold (none in this\n  set, but included for robustness).\n\nGrid-stride loop: `asc2.range(block_idx(), num_tiles, block_num(), unroll_factor=2)`.\nCores: `min(72, num_tiles)`. 72 vector cores on Ascend 950PR.\n\n## Tail handling\n\n```python\nn = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n```\nPassed as `real_shape=[n]` to both `copy_in` and `copy_out`. No host-side padding,\nno shape mutation. Ternary expression for the tail condition is supported syntax.\n\n## UB budget\n\nOp chain visible f32 tiles: `xf`, `neg_xf`, `exp_val`, `denom`, `result` = 5 tiles.\nWith `unroll_factor=2` and measured 1.6× overhead factor:\n\n| TILE | Estimated UB | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1024 | ~52K B | safe |\n| 2048 | ~156K B (measured: 155648) | safe |\n| 3072 | ~233K B | safe (< 253952) |\n| 4096 | ~311K B | **OVERFLOW** |\n\n**Selected: TILE=3072 (wide), TILE=1024 (narrow).** Both fit within the 253952 B\nbudget.\n\n## Numerical risks\n\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| `exp(-x)` overflow for large negative x | IEEE `inf` → `1/inf = 0`, matches golden |\n| f16 denormals near 0 | f32 promotion gives full mantissa range |\n| NaN propagation (case 13) | IEEE NaN passes through exp/div correctly |\n| ±inf (case 12) | bf16 ±inf → f32 ±inf → sigmoid gives 1.0/0.0 |\n| Catastrophic cancellation | None — no subtraction of near-equals in this formula |\n| Precision thresholds | f32 internal compute exceeds f16 threshold 2^-10, bf16 2^-7, f32 2^-13 |\n\n## Anti-cheat constraints\n\n- All math in `@asc2.jit` kernel on NPU\n- `torch` used only for: `torch.empty_like(x)`, `x.numel()`, `x.is_contiguous()`,\n  `x.contiguous()`, `x.dtype`\n- No `torch.sigmoid`, no tensor arithmetic, no `.to(dtype)` on device tensors\n- No output caching, no `data_ptr` reuse\n- Output is a fresh contiguous tensor with golden's exact shape/dtype\n\n## Host dispatch\n\n```python\ndef sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n    ensure_npu_platform()\n    x = x.contiguous() if not x.is_contiguous() else x\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    size = x.numel()\n    if size == 0: return out\n    if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n    else:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n    return out\n```\n\n## Syntax compliance (per pyasc-syntax-constraints)\n\n- `for` loop with `asc2.range()`, no `break`/`continue`/`return`\n- Ternary `if` for tail condition (supported)\n- No `print`, no imports, no `try/except`, no `lambda`, no `math.*`\n- Scalars on the right of tile operations (`exp_val + 1.0`, not `1.0 + exp_val`)\n- Device functions may `return`; kernel writes to output tensors\n- `asc.ConstExpr[int]` for `tile_size` parameter (compile-time)\n\n## Local validation ladder\n\n1. `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` — syntax gate only\n2. Worker static contract check — schema, imports, anti-cheat\n3. Exact-v2 compile gate for all 20 cases — `verified-local-compile` label\n4. **No numerical claims** without camodel (`verified-camodel`) or CANNBench\n   (`verified-cannbench`) execution\n\nDESIGN_DONE","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":155,"totalLines":155,"truncated":false}},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/design.md","time":{"start":1788347150765,"end":1788347150773}},"id":"prt_061cbf02d001K7uKcpfyzWDitx","sessionID":"ses_f9e3438bdffe7KqbdMk08DJgkW","messageID":"msg_061cbdf2c001fsJa2FKMB95ZK8"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347151216,"sessionID":"ses_f9e3438bdffe7KqbdMk08DJgkW","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_3e050b87c11042b6855f3f2f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/candidate.py"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/candidate.py</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: \"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n2: \n3: Kernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n4:   - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n5:   - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n6:   - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n7:     (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n8:   - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n9:     the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n10:     maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n11:   - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n12:     limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n13: \"\"\"\n14: \n15: import torch\n16: \n17: import asc\n18: import asc2\n19: \n20: from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n21: \n22: _WIDE_TILE = 3072\n23: _NARROW_TILE = 1024\n24: _MAX_CORES = 72\n25: \n26: \n27: @asc2.jit\n28: def _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n29:                     size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n30:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n31:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n32:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n33:                         unroll_factor=2):\n34:         off = t * tile_size\n35:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n36:         x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n37:         xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n38:         y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n39:         asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n40: \n41: \n42: def sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n43:     \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n44:     ensure_npu_platform()\n45:     if not x.is_contiguous():\n46:         x = x.contiguous()\n47:     out = torch.empty_like(x)\n48:     size = x.numel()\n49:     if size == 0:\n50:         return out\n51:     if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n52:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n53:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n54:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n55:     else:\n56:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n57:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n58:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n59:     return out\n\n(End of file - total 59 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n\nKernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n  - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n  - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n  - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n    (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n  - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n    the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n    maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n  - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n    limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport torch\n\nimport asc\nimport asc2\n\nfrom ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/candidate.py","text":"\"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n\nKernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n  - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n  - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n  - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n    (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n  - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n    the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n    maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n  - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n    limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport torch\n\nimport asc\nimport asc2\n\nfrom ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n\n_WIDE_TILE = 3072\n_NARROW_TILE = 1024\n_MAX_CORES = 72\n\n\n@asc2.jit\ndef _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n                    size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n    out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n    for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n                        unroll_factor=2):\n        off = t * tile_size\n        n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n        x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n        xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n        y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n        asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n\n\ndef sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n    ensure_npu_platform()\n    if not x.is_contiguous():\n        x = x.contiguous()\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    size = x.numel()\n    if size == 0:\n        return out\n    if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n    else:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n    return out","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":59,"totalLines":59,"truncated":false}},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140332/sigmoid-generate/candidate.py","time":{"start":1788347151209,"end":1788347151215}},"id":"prt_061cbf1e5001ep9e90giq72L4U","sessionID":"ses_f9e3438bdffe7KqbdMk08DJgkW","messageID":"msg_061cbdf2c001fsJa2FKMB95ZK8"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347151547,"sessionID":"ses_f9e3438bdffe7KqbdMk08DJgkW","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_02733fd392ab41de86267fed","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/operator-patterns.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/operator-patterns.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # CANNBench operator patterns\n2: \n3: These patterns describe the nine vendored operators. Task files remain the\n4: normative source.\n5: \n6: ## Common elementwise path\n7: \n8: `sigmoid`, `exp`, `mish`, and `gelu` flatten arbitrary-rank contiguous input to\n9: one dimension. Use grid-stride tiles, `real_shape=[n]` tails, f32 intermediates\n10: for f16/bf16 where needed, and `cores = min(72, num_tiles)`. Stable forms must\n11: preserve NaN and Inf behavior from `golden.py`. GELU has both `none` and `tanh`\n12: attributes and may require distinct kernels or tile widths.\n13: \n14: ## MaskedScale\n15: \n16: Cover every cross-product present in `cases.yaml`: x is f16/bf16/f32; mask is\n17: int8/uint8/f16/bf16/f32. Direct vector arithmetic on int8 is unsupported in the\n18: observed runtime; cast int8 to f16 and then f32. Torch uint8 can be reinterpreted\n19: as int8 without copying, but values 128..255 then require an in-kernel f32 +256\n20: correction. Avoid f16 and bf16 temporaries coexisting in one specialization;\n21: use a two-kernel f32 bridge for cross-half combinations.\n22: \n23: ## SwiGLU\n24: \n25: Split the selected even-sized dimension and compute `silu(x0) * x1`. Map the\n26: contiguous N-D tensor to `[outer, 2 * half_cols]` without materializing aligned\n27: halves. Two-dimensional copy operations require a 32-byte-aligned final tile\n28: dimension. Degenerate `half_cols` therefore needs a separately evidenced path.\n29: The current official result is 18/20: case 9 is an NPU runtime failure around\n30: the host narrow/contiguous fallback and case 12 is a bf16 NaN-position\n31: mismatch. Treat both roots as suspected until reproduced on hardware; a prior\n32: replacement fallback regressed to 0/20 and must not be copied.\n33: \n34: ## ForeachAddcdivScalar\n35: \n36: Loop over tensor lists on the host and launch one elementwise kernel for each\n37: triple. Preserve scalar zero, Inf, and NaN behavior. Compute division in f32\n38: for half inputs and cast once on output.\n39: \n40: ## ForeachNorm\n41: \n42: Return one 0-D result per input tensor. Specialize host dispatch by p, including\n43: negative p and Inf. Cross-core sum/max needs `asc2.atomic_add` or\n44: `asc2.atomic_max`; pad scalar storage to at least 32 bytes. A loop-carried\n45: accumulator must be seeded with a pyasc scalar/tile result, not a Python float.\n46: Use only `asc2.range` kwargs confirmed by the pinned source.\n47: \n48: ## RMSNorm\n49: \n50: Normalize each last-dimension row and multiply by gamma. The pinned v2 source\n51: contains `asc2.rms_norm`; the current canonical module uses it for the main\n52: path, with an f16 hop for bf16, and a manual fallback for shapes that fail its\n53: constraints. Preserve f32 accumulation where the manual path is used. Cover D\n54: from 2 through 8192, including prime/non-aligned tails and special values.\n55: \n\n(End of file - total 55 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# CANNBench operator patterns\n\nThese patterns describe the nine vendored operators. Task files remain the\nnormative source.\n\n## Common elementwise path\n\n`sigmoid`, `exp`, `mish`, and `gelu` flatten arbitrary-rank contiguous input to\none dimension. Use grid-stride tiles, `real_shape=[n]` tails, f32 intermediates\nfor f16/bf16 where needed, and `cores = min(72, num_tiles)`. Stable forms must\npreserve NaN and Inf behavior from `golden.py`. GELU has both `none` and `tanh`\nattributes and may require distinct kernels or tile widths.\n\n## MaskedScale\n\nCover every cross-product present in `cases.yaml`: x is f16/bf16/f32; mask is\nint8/uint8/f16/bf16/f32. Direct vector arithmetic on int8 is unsupported in the\nobserved runtime; cast int8 to f16 and then f32. Torch uint8 can be reinterpreted\nas int8 without copying, but values 128..255 then require an in-kernel f32 +256\ncorrection. Avoid f16 and bf16 temporaries coexisting in one specialization;","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/operator-patterns.md","text":"# CANNBench operator patterns\n\nThese patterns describe the nine vendored operators. Task files remain the\nnormative source.\n\n## Common elementwise path\n\n`sigmoid`, `exp`, `mish`, and `gelu` flatten arbitrary-rank contiguous input to\none dimension. Use grid-stride tiles, `real_shape=[n]` tails, f32 intermediates\nfor f16/bf16 where needed, and `cores = min(72, num_tiles)`. Stable forms must\npreserve NaN and Inf behavior from `golden.py`. GELU has both `none` and `tanh`\nattributes and may require distinct kernels or tile widths.\n\n## MaskedScale\n\nCover every cross-product present in `cases.yaml`: x is f16/bf16/f32; mask is\nint8/uint8/f16/bf16/f32. Direct vector arithmetic on int8 is unsupported in the\nobserved runtime; cast int8 to f16 and then f32. Torch uint8 can be reinterpreted\nas int8 without copying, but values 128..255 then require an in-kernel f32 +256\ncorrection. Avoid f16 and bf16 temporaries coexisting in one specialization;\nuse a two-kernel f32 bridge for cross-half combinations.\n\n## SwiGLU\n\nSplit the selected even-sized dimension and compute `silu(x0) * x1`. Map the\ncontiguous N-D tensor to `[outer, 2 * half_cols]` without materializing aligned\nhalves. Two-dimensional copy operations require a 32-byte-aligned final tile\ndimension. Degenerate `half_cols` therefore needs a separately evidenced path.\nThe current official result is 18/20: case 9 is an NPU runtime failure around\nthe host narrow/contiguous fallback and case 12 is a bf16 NaN-position\nmismatch. Treat both roots as suspected until reproduced on hardware; a prior\nreplacement fallback regressed to 0/20 and must not be copied.\n\n## ForeachAddcdivScalar\n\nLoop over tensor lists on the host and launch one elementwise kernel for each\ntriple. Preserve scalar zero, Inf, and NaN behavior. Compute division in f32\nfor half inputs and cast once on output.\n\n## ForeachNorm\n\nReturn one 0-D result per input tensor. Specialize host dispatch by p, including\nnegative p and Inf. Cross-core sum/max needs `asc2.atomic_add` or\n`asc2.atomic_max`; pad scalar storage to at least 32 bytes. A loop-carried\naccumulator must be seeded with a pyasc scalar/tile result, not a Python float.\nUse only `asc2.range` kwargs confirmed by the pinned source.\n\n## RMSNorm\n\nNormalize each last-dimension row and multiply by gamma. The pinned v2 source\ncontains `asc2.rms_norm`; the current canonical module uses it for the main\npath, with an f16 hop for bf16, and a manual fallback for shapes that fail its\nconstraints. Preserve f32 accumulation where the manual path is used. Cover D\nfrom 2 through 8192, including prime/non-aligned tails and special values.\n","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":55,"totalLines":55,"truncated":false}},"title":"skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/operator-patterns.md","time":{"start":1788347151541,"end":1788347151546}},"id":"prt_061cbf3a30016hVKrSTm3Pj2nJ","sessionID":"ses_f9e3438bdffe7KqbdMk08DJgkW","messageID":"msg_061cbdf2c001fsJa2FKMB95ZK8"}}
